    "explanation": "Article 51A was inserted into the Constitution by the 42nd Amendment (1976) on the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee. India's Constitution originally contained only rights (Parts III and IV) without corresponding duties for citizens. The addition of Fundamental Duties was inspired by the Constitution of the erstwhile Soviet Union (USSR) and represented a recognition that rights and duties are correlative — the enjoyment of rights by one person implies the observance of duties by others.\n\nUnlike Fundamental Rights (Part III), Fundamental Duties are not directly enforceable by courts. There is no constitutional mechanism for punishing a citizen who fails to observe these duties. However, they serve multiple important functions: (a) they act as a reminder to citizens that while enjoying rights, they must also be responsible members of society; (b) they provide an additional constitutional basis for legislative and executive action; (c) courts can use them as interpretive aids when adjudicating on the reasonableness of restrictions on fundamental rights; and (d) they can serve as a warning against anti-national and antisocial activities.\n\n**Clause (a) — Respect for Constitution, Flag, and Anthem:** This duty mandates loyalty to the constitutional framework. The Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971, criminalizes disrespect to the Constitution, National Flag, and National Anthem. However, this duty must be balanced with Article 19(1)(a) (freedom of expression) — the Supreme Court in Bijoe Emmanuel (1986) held that Jehovah's Witnesses who respectfully stood during the anthem but did not sing it were not violating this duty.\n\n**Clause (b) — Ideals of National Struggle:** Citizens must cherish the values of the independence movement — secularism, democracy, non-violence, social justice, and equality. This duty has moral rather than legal force but provides philosophical continuity between pre-independence ideals and post-independence governance.\n\n**Clause (c) — Sovereignty, Unity, and Integrity:** This duty provides constitutional support for laws against secession, terrorism, and activities that threaten national unity. It complements Articles 19(2) (reasonable restrictions on speech in the interests of sovereignty and integrity) and 356 (President's Rule when state governance breaks down).\n\n**Clause (d) — Defence of Country:** This duty obligates citizens to defend the nation and render national service when called upon. While India does not have compulsory military service, this duty would provide constitutional support for conscription if ever enacted.\n\n**Clause (e) — Harmony and Brotherhood; Dignity of Women:** This uniquely Indian duty combines two objectives — promoting national harmony transcending religious, linguistic, and regional divisions, and renouncing practices derogatory to women's dignity. The latter has been used to support legislation against dowry (Dowry Prohibition Act), domestic violence (Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005), and sexual harassment (POSH Act, 2013).\n\n**Clause (f) — Composite Culture:** India's heritage is described as \"composite\" — acknowledging that it is a synthesis of Hindu, Muslim, Sikh, Christian, Buddhist, Jain, tribal, and numerous other cultural traditions. This duty supports cultural preservation efforts and opposes cultural exclusivism.\n\n**Clause (g) — Environmental Protection:** This duty, along with Article 48A, forms the constitutional basis for environmental law. The Supreme Court has extensively relied on Article 51A(g) in environmental cases — M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (various), Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v. Union of India (1996), and numerous others. It has been read into Article 21 to establish the right to a clean environment.\n\n**Clause (h) — Scientific Temper:** Perhaps the most distinctive of the Fundamental Duties, this clause directs citizens to develop scientific temper, humanism, and the spirit of inquiry and reform. It reflects Jawaharlal Nehru's emphasis on rational thinking and modernization. It provides constitutional support for science education, opposition to superstition, and progressive social reform.\n\n**Clause (i) — Public Property and Non-Violence:** This duty opposes vandalism, destruction of public property during protests, and political violence. The Supreme Court in Destruction of Public and Private Properties v. State of AP (2009) directed that protest organizers should be held liable for property destruction.\n\n**Clause (j) — Striving for Excellence:** This aspirational duty directs citizens toward personal and collective excellence — a unique constitutional provision encouraging individual and national achievement.\n\n**Clause (k) — Parental Duty of Education (added 2002):** This clause, added along with Article 21A, makes it a fundamental duty of parents/guardians to provide education to children aged 6-14. It complements the right to education with a corresponding duty.",
